# Engineering conventions

> Prioritise correctness over visual polish. A plain interface showing a true number beats
> a beautiful one showing a plausible one.

## Every major component has

Tests · error handling · logging · authentication · authorisation · validation ·
documentation. A component missing one of these is not finished.

## Testing

- `tests/security/` holds the seven controls that must never regress. They are integration
  tests on purpose: a unit test of a helper proves the helper works, not that the control
  is wired into the path a request takes.
- Tests run offline against SQLite with no credentials, so there is never an excuse to
  skip them. Keep it that way: no test may require a network call or a vendor key.
- Prove the evaluators *discriminate*, not merely that they run. The flawed fixture agent
  exists so a passing evaluator can be distinguished from a lenient one.
- Assert the honest behaviour too: that a mock is labelled, that an excluded dimension is
  excluded, that an unanswerable question is refused.

## Error handling

- Raise a `FinagentError` subclass with a safe user message and useful `details`.
- Log the internal detail; return the envelope. Never leak a traceback, a SQL fragment or
  a library name.
- A failure in a logging call must never fail the operation it describes.

## Logging

- Structured, JSON by default, with correlation ids bound through `log_context`.
- Event names are snake_case nouns: `research_run_completed`, not `"Run finished!"`.
- Anything sensitive goes through `redact`.

## Comments

Write a comment only to state something the code cannot:

- **Why** a non-obvious choice was made (why the import guard is a namespace `__builtins__`
  and not a `builtins` deletion).
- A **constraint** a future reader would otherwise violate (why evidence with an unknown
  timestamp is excluded).
- A **correctness invariant** (why `history_for` excludes the traded bar).

Never narrate what the next line does. Never explain your change to the reviewer in a
comment — that belongs in the commit message.

## UI

Institutional research terminal, not a consumer SaaS site.

- Dense, high-contrast, restrained. Colour carries meaning (positive / negative / caution /
  simulated), never decoration.
- All figures are monospaced and tabular (`.numeric`) so columns line up and digits are
  comparable at a glance.
- Missing data renders as an em dash plus an explanation, never as zero and never as a
  guess.
- Every simulated or demo artefact carries its badge.
- Every destructive or expensive action says what it will cost or what it will remove
  before it runs.
- Server components fetch; server actions mutate; `"use client"` only where interaction
  genuinely requires it.

## Build order

Repository → architecture → database → auth → RBAC → agent schema → builder → model
abstraction → runner → traces → ingestion → RAG → citations → tasks → evaluation → golden
dataset → scoring → dashboard → observability → backtesting → cutoff → benchmarking →
experiments → multi-agent → enterprise.

Each layer must work before the next is started. A vertical slice that runs end to end is
worth more than four horizontal layers that do not.
